r Order No. 2, March 8, 1862"," ",["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$1b:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":12}]]}]}],["$","li","https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-presidents-general-war-order-no-3",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-presidents-general-war-order-no-3","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["American Presidency Project: President’s General War Order No. 3, March 8, 1862"," ",["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$1b:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":12}]]}]}],["$","li","https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-presidents-special-war-order-no-3",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-presidents-special-war-order-no-3","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["American Presidency Project: President’s Special War Order No. 3, March 11, 1862"," ",["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$1b:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":12}]]}]}],["$","li","https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-454",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-454","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["American Presidency Project: Lincoln’s March 13 directions for the Fort Monroe movement"," ",["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$1b:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":12}]]}]}],["$","li","https://history.army.mil/Research/Reference-Topics/Army-Campaigns/Brief-Summaries/Civil-War/",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"https://history.army.mil/Research/Reference-Topics/Army-Campaigns/Brief-Summaries/Civil-War/","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["U.S. Army Center of Military History: Civil War campaign summaries"," ",["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$1b:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":12}]]}]}],["$","li","https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo72995/pdf/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo72995.pdf",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo72995/pdf/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo72995.pdf","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Virginia Campaigns, March–August 1862"," ",["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$1b:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":12}]]}]}],["$","li","https://www.history.navy.mil/content/history/museums/hrnm/explore/the-battle-of-hampton-roads.html",{"children":"$L3b"}],"$L3c","$L3d","$L3e","$L3f","$L40","$L41"]}]]}]
20:["$","aside",null,{"className":"automation-note","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Best-effort automated guide"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"This guide was drafted from machine transcription and automated research, then checked before publication. Errors may remain. A public correction and suggestion workflow is planned."}]]}]
22:["$","div","The focus shifts to Confederate command",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1124,"label":"18:44"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"The focus shifts to Confederate command"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The episode compares Lincoln’s difficulties with McClellan to the long-running conflict between Jefferson Davis and Joseph E. Johnston over authority, communication, and military judgment."}]]}]]}]
23:["$","div","Johnston recommends withdrawal",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1254,"label":"20:54"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Johnston recommends withdrawal"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"At a February 19 meeting with Davis and the Confederate cabinet, Johnston argued that the Centreville–Manassas position would become untenable when roads and weather permitted McClellan to use his superior force."}]]}]]}]
24:["$","div","Johnston begins preparations without fixing a date with Davis",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1335,"label":"22:15"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Johnston begins preparations without fixing a date with Davis"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"After hearing that the withdrawal had already become a public rumor, Johnston began moving surplus stores and withheld his schedule from the Confederate government."}]]}]]}]
25:["$","div","Stores overwhelm the available transportation",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1426,"label":"23:46"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Stores overwhelm the available transportation"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Large stocks of baggage, rations, bacon, and salted meat had to move over a congested single-track railroad because muddy roads restricted wagon traffic."}]]}]]}]
26:["$","div","Johnston orders the retreat",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1532,"label":"25:32"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Johnston orders the retreat"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"On March 7 Johnston directed roughly 42,000 troops east of the Blue Ridge to move toward the Rappahannock. Jackson’s smaller Valley force remained near Winchester."}]]}]]}]
27:["$","div","The Manassas line is abandoned",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1555,"label":"25:55"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"The Manassas line is abandoned"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"By the evening of March 9 the Confederate infantry had left Centreville and Manassas. The rear guard destroyed rail equipment, buildings, and supplies that could not be removed; many heavy guns were left behind."}]]}]]}]
28:["$","div","The episode explains its title",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1578,"label":"26:18"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"The episode explains its title"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The hosts report the allegation that the smell of burning bacon helped alert United States scouts to the evacuation. The episode does not identify a primary witness for that specific claim."}]]}]]}]
29:["$","div","Lincoln questions the Urbanna plan again",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1697,"label":"28:17"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Lincoln questions the Urbanna plan again"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"According to McClellan’s account, Lincoln told him that critics portrayed the movement as a plot to expose Washington. McClellan assembled his twelve division commanders for a formal vote."}]]}]]}]
2a:["$","div","The division commanders vote eight to four",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1757,"label":"29:17"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"The division commanders vote eight to four"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Eight commanders supported the Urbanna operation and four opposed it. McClellan brought the commanders to the White House to report the result."}]]}]]}]
2b:["$","div","Lincoln imposes conditions on the movement",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1767,"label":"29:27"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Lincoln imposes conditions on the movement"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"President’s General War Order No. 3 required Washington to remain secure, restricted the initial movement until the lower Potomac was clear, required Army-Navy action against the river batteries, and set March 18 as the deadline to begin moving onto the Chesapeake."}]]}]]}]
2c:["$","div","Lincoln creates four army corps",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1814,"label":"30:14"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Lincoln creates four army corps"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"President’s General War Order No. 2 organized the active Army of the Potomac into corps under Irvin McDowell, Edwin V. Sumner, Samuel P. Heintzelman, and Erasmus D. Keyes."}]]}]]}]
2d:["$","div","CSS Virginia threatens the water route",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1860,"label":"31:00"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"CSS Virginia threatens the water route"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Virginia attacked the United States blockading squadron in Hampton Roads on March 8. Monitor fought the Confederate ironclad on March 9, preventing Virginia from completing the destruction but leaving an operational threat in place."}]]}]]}]
2e:["$","div","Washington confirms Johnston’s withdrawal",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1900,"label":"31:40"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Washington confirms Johnston’s withdrawal"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"News of the abandoned Centreville–Manassas line reached McClellan while he met with Lincoln and Stanton. He crossed the Potomac to organize a pursuit."}]]}]]}]
2f:["$","div","The Army of the Potomac marches to empty works",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":1948,"label":"32:28"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"The Army of the Potomac marches to empty works"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"On March 10 United States columns entered Centreville and Manassas, finding burned stores, abandoned camps, and wooden Quaker guns. The evidence contradicted McClellan’s estimate of 150,000 Confederates in the position."}]]}]]}]
30:["$","div","McClellan loses the general-in-chief role",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":2003,"label":"33:23"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"McClellan loses the general-in-chief role"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Lincoln’s March 11 order relieved McClellan of command over the other military departments while leaving him in command of the Department and Army of the Potomac."}]]}]]}]
31:["$","div","The Urbanna operation becomes impractical",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":2154,"label":"35:54"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"The Urbanna operation becomes impractical"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Johnston’s new position near the Rappahannock could rapidly oppose a landing at Urbanna. McClellan therefore abandoned that landing point rather than advance overland from Manassas."}]]}]]}]
32:["$","div","McClellan proposes Fort Monroe",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":2191,"label":"36:31"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"McClellan proposes Fort Monroe"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"McClellan asked his four corps commanders to approve a transfer farther down the Chesapeake to Fort Monroe, followed by an advance up the Virginia Peninsula toward Richmond."}]]}]]}]
33:["$","div","The Peninsula movement begins",{"className":"moment","children":[["$","$L21",null,{"seconds":2341,"label":"39:01"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"The Peninsula movement begins"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"On March 17 the first Army of the Potomac division embarked at Alexandria for Fort Monroe, one day before the deadline in Lincoln’s March 8 order."}]]}]]}]
34:["$","div","Secretary of War",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Secretary of War"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Judah P. Benjamin"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Civilian head of the Confederate War Department during the February 19 strategy conference and the preparations for withdrawal"}]]}]
35:["$","div","Department of Northern Virginia and principal field army near Manassas",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Department of Northern Virginia and principal field army near Manassas"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Joseph E. Johnston"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Ordered the March 7 withdrawal from Centreville and Manassas and established a new defensive position behind the Rappahannock"}]]}]
36:["$","div","Valley District",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Valley District"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Commanded the approximately 5,400 troops left near Winchester rather than included in Johnston’s east-of-the-Blue-Ridge withdrawal"}]]}]
37:["$","div","Cavalry brigade and rear guard",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Cavalry brigade and rear guard"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"J. E. B. Stuart"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Covered Johnston’s withdrawal from the Bull Run line after the infantry departed"}]]}]
38:["$","div","Flag officer commanding CSS Virginia on March 8",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Flag officer commanding CSS Virginia on March 8"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Franklin Buchanan"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Led Virginia’s attack on the United States blockading squadron before being wounded; command passed to Lieutenant Catesby ap Roger Jones for the March 9 engagement with Monitor"}]]}]
39:["$","h3",null,{"className":"subsection-title","children":"Other people heard or discussed"}]
3a:["$","div",null,{"className":"people-grid compact-people","children":[["$","article","George B. McClellan",{"className":"person-card","children":[["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep129-george-mcclellan-1861.jpg","alt":"Portrait of George B. McClellan","width":420,"height":520,"loading":"lazy"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"George B. McClellan"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Directed the Army of the Potomac’s preliminary operations and changed its destination from Urbanna to Fort Monroe after Johnston withdrew"}]]}]]}],["$","article","Abraham Lincoln",{"className":"person-card","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"portrait-placeholder","children":["$","span",null,{"children":"AL"}]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Abraham Lincoln"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Approved the Chesapeake movement with conditions, imposed the four-corps structure, and removed McClellan from the general-in-chief role"}]]}]]}],["$","article","Edwin M. Stanton",{"className":"person-card","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"portrait-placeholder","children":["$","span",null,{"children":"EM"}]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Edwin M. Stanton"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Managed the War Department, publicly demanded offensive action, and became the direct reporting authority for military departments after March 11"}]]}]]}],["$","article","Randolph B. Marcy",{"className":"person-card","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"portrait-placeholder","children":["$","span",null,{"children":"RB"}]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Randolph B. Marcy"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Served as McClellan’s chief of staff and received Lincoln’s criticism after the Harpers Ferry bridge plan failed"}]]}]]}],["$","article","Irvin McDowell",{"className":"person-card","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"portrait-placeholder","children":["$","span",null,{"children":"IM"}]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Irvin McDowell"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Voted against the Urbanna plan and was assigned command of I Corps by Lincoln"}]]}]]}],["$","article","Edwin V. Sumner",{"className":"person-card","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"portrait-placeholder","children":["$","span",null,{"children":"EV"}]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Edwin V. Sumner"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Voted against the Urbanna plan and was assigned command of II Corps by Lincoln"}]]}]]}],["$","article","Samuel P. Heintzelman",{"className":"person-card","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"portrait-placeholder","children":["$","span",null,{"children":"SP"}]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Samuel P. Heintzelman"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Voted against the Urbanna plan and was assigned command of III Corps by Lincoln"}]]}]]}],["$","article","Erasmus D. Keyes",{"className":"person-card","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"portrait-placeholder","children":["$","span",null,{"children":"ED"}]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Erasmus D. Keyes"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Supported the waterborne concept subject to conditions and was assigned command of IV Corps"}]]}]]}],["$","article","Joseph Hooker",{"className":"person-card","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"portrait-placeholder","children":["$","span",null,{"children":"JH"}]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Joseph Hooker"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Prepared a division-sized operation against the Confederate batteries blockading the lower Potomac"}]]}]]}],["$","article","Jefferson Davis",{"className":"person-card","children":["$L42","$L43"]}],"$L44","$L45","$L46","$L47"]}]
